entry.content:
add_website: inputSchema  parameters: +input.follow_redirect input.follow_redirect description: Consent to follow a redirect to ANOTHER site. The server follows the URL's redirects (up to 5, never to a private address) when you gave no start_url: within one site (http to https, apex to www) it just registers the final URL; to a different registrable domain it answers status "redirected" with the destination and creates nothing until you call again with follow_redirect: true. The original host is kept as an alias. A loop, a refused hop or a timeout registers the URL as sent and says why in redirect.
get_audit_status: description  +health_score, issues_found and report_id stay null while the status is queued, pending or running: a run that is still publishing has no fetchable report yet. A run that ended (completed, failed or cancelled) returns the ones it has, so a failed run with a published notice report still carries its report_id.
get_issue: description  +Severity has three scales: a rule's severity (error, warning or info: get_rule, `ruleSeverity` on report rows, and `rule_severity` on issues; the severity in the llm and markdown report formats is this one too), a single check result's severity (error when the check failed, warning when it warned: `severity` on get_report summary rows, so a rule declared warning can still show an error row), and an issue's severity (list_issues and get_issue `severity`): for an issue the audit created it is high, medium or low, mapped from the rule's severity as error to high, warning to medium, info to low; 
get_report: description  +Severity has three scales: a rule's severity (error, warning or info: get_rule, `ruleSeverity` on report rows, and `rule_severity` on issues; the severity in the llm and markdown report formats is this one too), a single check result's severity (error when the check failed, warning when it warned: `severity` on get_report summary rows, so a rule declared warning can still show an error row), and an issue's severity (list_issues and get_issue `severity`): for an issue the audit created it is high, medium or low, mapped from the rule's severity as error to high, warning to medium, info to low; 
get_rule: description  +Severity has three scales: a rule's severity (error, warning or info: get_rule, `ruleSeverity` on report rows, and `rule_severity` on issues; the severity in the llm and markdown report formats is this one too), a single check result's severity (error when the check failed, warning when it warned: `severity` on get_report summary rows, so a rule declared warning can still show an error row), and an issue's severity (list_issues and get_issue `severity`): for an issue the audit created it is high, medium or low, mapped from the rule's severity as error to high, warning to medium, info to low; 
list_audits: description  -(#1700): +(#1700), and only once the run has ended: a queued, pending or running run shows null for health_score, issues_found and report_id, as get_audit_status does:
list_issues: description  -Filter by status/severity/category to narrow down. +Severity has three scales: a rule's severity (error, warning or info: get_rule, `ruleSeverity` on report rows, and `rule_severity` on issues; the severity in the llm and markdown report formats is this one too), a single check result's severity (error when the check failed, warning when it warned: `severity` on get_report summary rows, so a rule declared warning can still show an error row), and an issue's severity (list_issues and get_issue `severity`): for an issue the audit created it is high, medium or low, mapped from the rule's severit
list_websites: description  +name (`name`: the display name, else the title the site gives itself, else the domain),
run_audit: inputSchema  parameters: +input.follow_redirect input.follow_redirect description: Consent to follow a redirect to ANOTHER site. The server follows the URL's redirects (up to 5, never to a private address) when you gave no start_url: within one site (http to https, apex to www) it just registers the final URL; to a different registrable domain it answers status "redirected" with the destination and creates nothing until you call again with follow_redirect: true. The original host is kept as an alias. A loop, a refused hop or a timeout registers the URL as sent and says why in redirect.

